    "evidence_text": "ScRNN with Backoff\tWe now describe semi-character RNNs for word recognition, explain their limitations, and suggest techniques to improve them.\n\nBelow, we explore different ways to back off when the ScRNN predicts UNK (a frequent outcome for rare and unseen words): Pass-through:\n\nBackoff to background model: We also consider falling back upon a more generic word recognition model trained upon a larger, less-specialized corpus whenever the foreground word recognition model predicts UNK.\n\nThe ScRNN model with pass-through backoff offers better protection, bringing back the adversarial accuracy within $5\\%$ range for the swap attack.\n\nWe observe additional gains by using background models as a backoff alternative, because of its lower word error rate (WER), especially,\n\nAs we shall see in section \u00a7 \"Understanding Model Sensitivity\" , the backoff neutral variation has the lowest sensitivity due to mapping UNK predictions to a fixed neutral word.\n\nWhile the neutral backoff model is effective on 2-char attacks, it hurts performance in the no attack setting, since it incorrectly\n\nWe see this trade-off in Figure 2 , where we plot WER and sensitivity on the two axes, and depict the robustness when using different backoff variants.",
